From 496732be450300a6312b253074e2022874118f72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 09:21:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] lane-195: multi-segment flash attention - the ggml API + CPU
 reference + its gate

ggml_flash_attn_ext_add_segments() attaches up to 2 extra K/V segments (src[5..8], count in
op_params[4]) that are read IN PLACE at their own dtype and folded into ONE fp32 online softmax.
This is what lets a KV cache with an exact F16 ring beside a quantized body skip materialising the
window entirely: the concat that forced one dtype on both operands is the whole cost the precision
tail pays (596.58 MiB of scratch at 131K, plus the body-leg -0.5/-0.6 pp F16 cannot avoid).

CPU reference: the segment loop sits OUTSIDE the (S, M, VKQ) accumulator in
flash_attn_ext_f16_one_chunk, so it is one continued online softmax rather than two attentions
glued together. Mask bases are DERIVED from each segment's ne[1] and can never disagree with the
tensors. The KV-chunked and tiled CPU paths are refused when segments are present - both assume the
window lives entirely in src[1]/src[2] and would have attended over the body only: green, and wrong.
With no segments every line reduces to the shipped path.

Gate (tests/test-fa-segments.cpp, CPU only, zero GPU time), all as expected:
  LEG 1 split-equivalence, 6 awkward splits          nmse = 0 EXACTLY (bit-identical)
  LEG 2 negative control, segment rows shifted by 1  nmse 0.031-0.282, fires on all 5
  LEG 3 q4_0 body + F16 ring vs an INDEPENDENT       nmse 3e-9..1.2e-6 against tol 5e-4
        plain-softmax reference computed in-file

Two defects in my own first draft of that gate, both found by running it and both recorded in the
file: LEG 1 compared an F16 accumulator against a forced-F32 one and so measured the accumulator
(~2e-6) rather than the indexing; and the first negative control shifted segment SIZES, which moved
the mask bases with them and was arithmetically a no-op. Corrected controls are what produced the
numbers above.
